For simplicity, we omit $\pmb{x}$ for $q$ in subsequent elaboration. With the model $p_{\theta}$ and the target $\pmb{y}_0$ , we interpolate $\pmb{y}_0$ and $p_{\theta}(\pmb{y}_{T - 1}|\pmb{y}_T,\pmb{x})$ to construct intermediate distributions whose numbers of modalities are between the modalities in $\pmb{y}_0$ and those captured by $p_{\theta}$ :

$$
\begin{array}{l} q \left(\boldsymbol {y} _ {t} \mid \boldsymbol {y} _ {0}\right) := \left(\gamma_ {t} \mathbf {1} + (1 - \gamma_ {t}) \boldsymbol {y} _ {0}\right) \odot \mathcal {P} _ {t - 1} / Z _ {t} \\ := \left(\gamma_ {t} \mathcal {P} _ {t - 1} + (1 - \gamma_ {t}) \mathcal {P} _ {t - 1} \odot \boldsymbol {y} _ {0}\right) / Z _ {t}, \tag {8} \\ \end{array}
$$

where $\mathcal{P}_{t - 1} = p_{\theta}(\pmb{y}_{t - 1}|\pmb{y}_t,\pmb{x})$ $\pmb{y}_0$ is the one-hot vector sequence of the target in data, $\gamma_t\in (0,1]$ is the hyper-parameter for controlling the interpolation, $\odot$ represents element-wise multiplication and $Z_{t}\in (0,1]^{n}$ is the factor for normalization. We can consider the definition of Eq. 8 as data corruption on the modality probability landscape of $\mathcal{P}$ by extracting the $\mathcal{P}$ term out of the parenthesis. To ensure the form in Eq. 8 holds for $t\geq 1$ (See Appendix B), we define $q(\pmb{y}_{t - 1}|\pmb{y}_t,\pmb{y}_0)$ as:

$$
\begin{array}{l} q \left(\boldsymbol {y} _ {t - 1} \mid \boldsymbol {y} _ {t}, \boldsymbol {y} _ {0}\right) := \gamma_ {t - 1} \mathcal {P} _ {t - 2} + \left((1 - \gamma_ {t - 1}) \right. \tag {9} \\ \left. \mathcal {P} _ {t - 2} - \omega_ {t} \mathcal {P} _ {t - 1}\right) \odot \boldsymbol {y} _ {0} + \omega_ {t} Z _ {t} \boldsymbol {y} _ {0} \odot \boldsymbol {y} _ {t} \\ \end{array}
$$

Here, $\omega_{t}$ is a hyper-parameter. Similarly, we also re-normalize $q(\pmb{y}_{t-1}|\pmb{y}_t,\pmb{y}_0)$ . Intuitively, the sum of the first term with $\mathcal{P}_{t-2}$ and the second term with $\pmb{y}_0$ performs the modality interpolation as in Eq. 8, and adding the third term with $\pmb{y}_0\odot \pmb{y}_t$ attempts to preserve the modalities captured by $\pmb{y}_t$ in the denoising transitions. Thus, $\pmb{y}_{t-1}$ serves as a intermediate target, with fewer modalities than $\pmb{y}_0$ but more modalities than $\pmb{y}_t$ , for smoothing the modality learning.

